{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791365301,"nonce":"grHJHkw2aPtVXhoS//Db5LCij8M=","title":"Generic scalar specialization reads Dynamic arguments as raw numeric bits","message":"At 76d0da500faee5df0c55d82c08d855d5d23b65fd, same\u003cT\u003e(a:T,b:T):Bool with a Dynamic first argument and a concrete Float second argument selects the Float specialization. The Dynamic box reaches a scalar slot and its address is read as f64 bits. The Dynamic-array read fix exposes this when unit.Test.eq compares x[0] with -1.5 in TestOps.\n\nReproducer: haxe-conformance/regressions/GenericDynamicArguments.hx. Native Haxe --interp passes same(number:Dynamic = 1.5, 1.5) in either argument order; same(text:Dynamic = \"0\", 0.0) is false, preserving value tags. show\u003cT\u003e(a:T,b:T):String must stringify the Dynamic payload. A call returning T keeps its inferred result: Context.typeof(GenericDynamicArguments.pick(text,0.0)) is Float. Native artifact: /tmp/rayzor-generic-dynamic-arguments-native.log.\n\nRejected approaches: converting all Dynamic operands to the inferred Float fixes numeric pairs but makes the Dynamic String \"0\" compare equal to Float 0.0. Overriding all inferred T bindings with Dynamic changes the logical type of calls returning T; /tmp/rayzor-generic-dynamic-type/GenericDynamicType.hx establishes native Haxe infers Float in both argument orders. Preserve that inferred result and use a boxed specialization only when the result does not depend on the type parameter. Concrete arguments must then be boxed for that specialization. Nullable-number and UInt inference (969a6389b7c9370c509cd39ac19090874b6bc34c8f613415458efd74d30dae38) are separate unresolved cases.","files":null}]}